// Cheat (a.k.a. BS, "I Doubt It") — stress-test DSL encoding (NOT part of the
// canonical corpus).
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/beating/cheat.html (Pagat).
//
// Rules as fetched: one standard 52-card deck dealt out (as evenly as
// possible) among 2-10 players. Turn order is clockwise. On a turn, a player
// discards one or more cards FACE DOWN onto a shared pile and announces a
// rank for them; the player does NOT have to actually hold that rank — lying
// is the whole game. The claimed rank is not a free choice: "the first player
// must discard Aces, the second player discards Twos, the next player
// Threes, and so on... after Tens come Jacks, then Queens, then Kings, then
// back to Aces" — a fixed ascending cycle tied to the running count of plays
// made so far (NOT reset by a challenge; play "continues clockwise from the
// next player in sequence, with ranks advancing as normal"). Any other player
// may call "Cheat!"; the challenged play is turned face up. If every card
// matches the announced rank, the challenger takes the whole discard pile
// into their hand; otherwise the player who played them does. The first
// player to shed their whole hand AND survive any challenge on that final
// play wins.
//
// === Simplifications / deliberate cuts (declared, not hidden) ===
//
// 1. Players fixed to 3..4 (Pagat allows 2-10). Chosen to match the
//    range this stress-test task specified; nothing else about the
//    encoding depends on the exact count and it generalizes to any N >= 3
//    the round/auction machinery below is written generically over `players`.
// 2. A play discards 1-4 cards (a common house-rule cap), not Pagat's
//    unbounded "one or more cards" (a player could, in principle, dump an
//    entire suit of 13 at once). This keeps the `choose integer in 1..cap`
//    decision's action space small and finite, which the target runtime
//    (OpenSpiel) needs anyway; raising the cap to `hand_size` would be a
//    one-line change (see the `cap` binding below) with no other
//    consequence.
// 3. Challenge arbitration (GAP, major — see inline `// GAP:` below). Pagat's
//    "any player may challenge" is a real-time race: whoever shouts "Cheat!"
//    first gets to challenge, and in practice it is usually the very next
//    player to act who is best-placed to notice a miscount. The DSL's
//    decision model has no construct for a genuinely simultaneous
//    multi-player reaction (matching the target runtime, OpenSpiel, whose
//    decision nodes are sequential) — Coup's analogous "challenge window" is
//    still a Python `instantiate` mechanic in this repo's corpus
//    (docs/decisions.md, "Interactive decisions": "the challenge / block /
//    climbing vocabulary" is explicitly listed as not yet built as a shared
//    library form). So this file rolls its own challenge window on the
//    *auction* form of the kernel `round` (`round offering [call_cheat,
//    pass_cheat] from claimant offset_by left over players where player !=
//    claimant ...`), exactly the way Stud rolled its own betting round before
//    a shared `betting` library form existed. This turns "whoever reacts
//    fastest" into "the claimant's left-hand neighbour gets first refusal,
//    then clockwise around the table" — a full seat-order priority queue
//    instead of a race. This changes the game's texture (seat position now
//    matters for who gets to catch a lie) but preserves every other rule
//    exactly: anyone still gets a chance to challenge every single play, and
//    the outcome (pile goes to the liar or the challenger) is identical.
//
// See the bottom of this file for the numbered stdlib-language findings this
// encoding surfaced (visibility overrides, epistemic ops, move-type
// parameter domains, `rotate`, `winner:`) — none blocked the encoding, all
// have a documented workaround in the body below at the site they bit.

game Cheat {

  players: 3..4
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52

  zones {
    deck        : Deck          // only used once, at setup
    hand[player]: Hand<player>  // identity to owner, count_only to others
    last_play   : FaceDownPile  // this turn's face-down discard, pre-adjudication
    reveal_area : Discard       // face-up staging zone: a challenge exposes last_play here
    pile        : FaceDownPile  // the accumulated, never-revealed, unchallenged discards
  }

  // `required_rank`/`turn`/`claimant`/`challenger`/`passes` are ordinary state,
  // hence PUBLIC to every player by the language's own convention (see
  // cardlang/openspiel/infostate.py: "state variables... public by
  // convention: hidden information lives only in zones"). That is exactly
  // the epistemic status the rank claim needs — everyone at the table always
  // knows what rank is being claimed and who is being challenged; the only
  // thing that must stay hidden is the true identity of the cards in
  // `last_play`/`pile`, which is zone-visibility's job, not state's.
  state {
    turn          : Player  = 0     // whose turn to play
    claimant      : Player  = 0     // who made the play currently up for challenge
    challenger    : Player? = none  // who called "Cheat!" this turn, if anyone
    passes        : Integer = 0     // challenge-window responses so far this turn
    required_rank : String  = "A"   // the rank this turn's play must claim to be
    score[player] : Integer = 0     // 1 for the winner, 0 otherwise (see finding #5)
  }

  phase setup {
    shuffle deck
    deal all cards from deck as-equally-as-possible to each hand
  }

  phase play {
    // Ends the instant somebody sheds out and survives the resulting
    // challenge window (or draws no challenge at all) — see the `score`
    // assignment below, which only fires when `hand[claimant]` truly ends
    // the turn empty.
    repeat until (any player p: score[p] == 1) {
      claimant   := turn
      challenger := none
      passes     := 0

      // --- the play: `turn` discards 1-4 cards face down, claim implied by
      // `required_rank` (state, hence already public — no announcement
      // needed). The actual cards chosen are a private "chosen" selection;
      // wrapping in `for each player p: if p == turn` is what binds `actor`
      // for that selection (see cardlang/runtime/execute.py: a `chosen`
      // movement requires `ctx.require_actor`, which only a move_type effect
      // or a `for each` binder supplies) — the same idiom French Tarot uses
      // for its taker-only chien discard.
      for each player p: if p == turn {
        let hand_size = count over hand[p] as c: true
        let cap = if hand_size < 4 then hand_size else 4
        let n = choose integer in 1 .. cap
        move chosen n cards from hand[p] to last_play
      }
      // No visibility override needed on the movement above (see finding #1
      // below): `hand[p]` is `identity to owner`, so `turn`'s own observation
      // of *that* movement already carries the true identities through the
      // SOURCE side, regardless of `last_play`'s own `count_only to all`
      // declaration — the actor always privately knows what they really
      // played, other players only ever learn the count.

      // --- the challenge window: a priority queue starting at claimant's
      // left, going once around; the first "Cheat!" ends it immediately,
      // otherwise it ends after everyone else has had one chance to pass.
      // See finding #3 (GAP, major) above: this is a seat-ordered queue
      // standing in for Pagat's real-time race.
      round offering [call_cheat, pass_cheat]
            from claimant offset_by left
            over players where player != claimant
            until challenger is not none
               or passes >= (number of players where player != claimant)

      if challenger is not none {
        // Reveal: moving last_play's (hidden) cards into `reveal_area`
        // (`identity to all`) makes every observer's projection of the
        // DESTINATION `identity`, regardless of the source's own
        // `count_only` declaration — see decisions.md, "Observation
        // events": "the destination projection updates... Observers learn
        // what arrived if their projection of B reveals it." That is
        // exactly Pagat's "the challenged player's cards are revealed," with
        // no bespoke `reveal` operation needed (see finding #2, GAP, major,
        // below — the grammar does not actually expose a `reveal` statement,
        // so this zone-routing trick is the real mechanism, not a shortcut
        // around one).
        move all cards from last_play to reveal_area
        let claim_true =
          (sum over reveal_area as c: if c.rank == required_rank then 1 else 0)
            == (count over reveal_area as c: true)
        if claim_true {
          // The claim held: the challenger was wrong and takes everything.
          move all cards from reveal_area to hand[challenger]
          move all cards from pile to hand[challenger]
        } else {
          // Caught cheating: the liar takes everything back.
          move all cards from reveal_area to hand[claimant]
          move all cards from pile to hand[claimant]
        }
      } else {
        // Nobody challenged: the play joins the pile, still never revealed
        // to anyone but the player who made it (who already privately knows,
        // per the source-side observation noted above).
        move all cards from last_play to pile
      }

      // `claimant`'s hand can only be empty here if they truly shed their
      // whole hand AND survived the challenge (a caught lie always refills
      // it via the `pile`/`reveal_area` move above) — so this one check is
      // exactly Pagat's "first to get rid of all their cards and survive any
      // challenge resulting from their final play."
      if hand[claimant] is empty { score[claimant] := 1 }

      required_rank := next_claim_rank(required_rank)
      turn := turn offset_by left
    }
  }

  // See finding #5 (below): `winner:` only takes `rank_dir NAME`, not a raw
  // player expression the way `loser:` does — `score` is a 0/1 proxy for
  // "is the winner", the same shape Coup's `winner: highest alive` already
  // uses in this corpus for an elimination-style result.
  winner: highest score
}

// === Cheat-local move vocabulary: the challenge window ===
//
// Both are always legal for any player still in the challenge ring (no
// `when:` guard needed — matches Bridge's/Pinochle's unconditional `pass`
// move types, which likewise omit `when:` entirely).

move_type call_cheat {
  effect { challenger := actor }
}

move_type pass_cheat {
  effect { passes := passes + 1 }
}

// === Cheat-local stdlib: the fixed ascending claim cycle ===
//
// Pagat's sequence (A, 2, 3, ... 10, J, Q, K, back to A) is exactly the
// built-in `rotate <var> through [...]` state-cycle primitive's job — except
// `rotate`'s value list is grammatically `name_list: NAME ("," NAME)*`, bare
// NAME tokens only, and numeric ranks 2-10 lex as INT, not NAME (see finding
// #4, minor, below). A plain `elif` chain over string rank labels (already
// the corpus's own idiom for rank comparisons — Cribbage/French
// Tarot/Pinochle all write `c.rank == "K"` etc. as bare string literals)
// reproduces the identical cycle with no engine change.
function next_claim_rank(r: String) =
  if   r == "A"  then "2"
  elif r == "2"  then "3"
  elif r == "3"  then "4"
  elif r == "4"  then "5"
  elif r == "5"  then "6"
  elif r == "6"  then "7"
  elif r == "7"  then "8"
  elif r == "8"  then "9"
  elif r == "9"  then "10"
  elif r == "10" then "J"
  elif r == "J"  then "Q"
  elif r == "Q"  then "K"
  else "A"

// === Language findings this encoding surfaced ===
//
// 1. (major) Per-move `visibility:` override is unimplemented at runtime.
//    decisions.md ("Per-observer visibility on moves") documents a move-level
//    `visibility: { observer: projection, ... }` clause that overrides a
//    zone's declared projection for specific observers, with a worked
//    example. The grammar parses it (`vis_clause`) and the type checker
//    walks it (typecheck.py:535), but `cardlang/runtime/observe.py`'s
//    `movement()` — the ONLY place per-observer projections are actually
//    emitted — reads solely `zone_projection(...)` off the zone's static
//    declaration; the parsed `Movement.visibility` field is never read
//    anywhere in cardlang/runtime/. A move-level override is therefore dead
//    syntax today: writing one would parse and typecheck but have zero
//    runtime effect on what any observer learns. This did not block Cheat —
//    the zone-declaration-only model turns out to be expressive enough (the
//    actor's own hand already leaks identity to them through the SOURCE side
//    of the movement, per the comment at the `move chosen n cards...` site
//    above) — but a game that genuinely needed a DIFFERENT projection for
//    the SAME zone depending on which move touched it (not just "the mover
//    happens to also own the source zone") would hit a real, verified gap.
// 2. (major) The epistemic operation vocabulary is mostly unimplemented.
//    decisions.md's "Stdlib memory-affecting operations" table specs
//    `peek`/`reveal`/`hide`/`shuffle`/`announce`/`expose_top`/`forget` as
//    prose statements. The grammar's `epistemic_op` production
//    (cardlang/grammar/cardlang.lark:191) accepts only `shuffle zone_expr` —
//    none of the other six parse as statements at all. Cheat needed exactly
//    "reveal the challenged play to everyone," and the workaround (route the
//    cards through an `identity to all` zone — see the `reveal_area` move
//    above) turned out to be a clean, arguably MORE honest expression of the
//    same idea (it is literally what a `reveal` builtin would have to do
//    internally, per the zone-projection model), so this is not a blocker
//    for card-content reveals. It would block a game that needed to reveal a
//    STATE variable's value to a subset of players (`announce`'s documented
//    job) rather than all players uniformly, since state is unconditionally
//    public to every player already (cardlang/openspiel/infostate.py) with
//    no per-observer override — there is no way in the current language to
//    make a state fact known to *some but not all* players without passing
//    it through a zone.
// 3. (major) Move-type parameter domains only support `Suit`/`Suit?`.
//    `enumerate_domain()` (cardlang/runtime/mechanics.py:223) is the function
//    that expands a parameterized `move_type`'s declared domain into the
//    flat per-turn candidate list every `round offering [...]` needs; it
//    handles exactly one case (`Suit`, optionally nullable) and raises
//    `NotImplementedError` for anything else. This blocked modeling the
//    claimed rank OR the card count as a move-type parameter (e.g. a
//    Bridge-`submit_bid`-style `move_type play(claimed_rank: Rank, count:
//    Integer)`) two different ways: `Rank` isn't `Suit`, and move_type_def
//    only ever takes ONE parameter anyway (`move_param` in the grammar is a
//    single `"(" NAME ":" payload_type ")"`, not a list). The workaround —
//    plain phase-body statements (`choose integer`, a deterministic `state`
//    cycle) instead of the `round offering` vocabulary for the main play
//    decision — works fully but means the "declare your claim" step is not
//    expressed as a first-class move_type the way Bridge's bid is; it is
//    ordinary imperative code, the same shape Cribbage's/French Tarot's
//    non-round mechanics already use for the parts that do not fit a
//    `round`.
// 4. (minor) `rotate`'s cycle list is NAME-only, so it cannot hold numeric
//    ranks. See the `next_claim_rank` function above and its comment;
//    the workaround (a hand-written `elif` chain) is functionally
//    identical to what `rotate` would do, just not reusing the primitive.
// 5. (minor) `winner:` cannot take a direct player-valued expression the way
//    `loser:` can (grammar: `winner: "winner" ":" rank_dir NAME` vs.
//    `loser: "loser" ":" expr`). Cheat's result is "this one specific
//    player, full stop" (like Coup's last-survivor result), not a ranked
//    score, so it needs the same `score`-proxy workaround Coup's `winner:
//    highest alive` already uses elsewhere in this corpus — not a Cheat-
//    specific problem, but a real asymmetry between the two clauses worth
//    flagging since nothing else in the grammar explains why `loser:` is
//    strictly more expressive than `winner:`.
